WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:02.834
-Our next guest is a very
talented actress, comedian,

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:05.167
and cabaret performer you know
from shows such as

00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:07.067
"Inside Amy Schumer"
and "Lady Dynamite."

00:00:07.067 --> 00:00:09.601
She stars in the new comedy
series "Somebody Somewhere."

00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:12.567
New episodes are available
Sundays on HBO and HBO Max.

00:00:12.567 --> 00:00:14.133
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:15.667
-Are we still going to be
tailing Rick then?

00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:17.067
'Cause I have some stuff.

00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:19.167
-No, he'll be
at my parents' house.

00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:20.734
-Well, then, you're got to
be there.

00:00:20.734 --> 00:00:22.968
-No, I have better things to do.

00:00:22.968 --> 00:00:25.701
Saturdays are actually
pretty big for me.

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:28.067
-Oh, really?
You got like a Saturday ritual?

00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:29.601
-I do.
-Oh.

00:00:29.601 --> 00:00:32.901
-I like to sit down and write
out a list of goals for the week

00:00:32.901 --> 00:00:35.801
and then, of course, I write
down a list of accomplishments

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:37.767
for the previous week
and stuff like that.

00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:40.133
-Oh, my God. That's so amazing.

00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:41.467
-Yeah.

00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:43.100
I don't do any of that.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.167
I like to lay around
drinking wine in my underwear.

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:47.133
-Oh.
-It's pretty [bleep] great.

00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:48.534
-Hmm.

00:00:48.534 --> 00:00:50.834
-Please welcome back to the show
Bridget Everett, everybody!

00:00:50.834 --> 00:00:53.434
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:53.434 --> 00:01:00.133
♪♪

00:01:00.133 --> 00:01:06.767
♪♪

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:09.200
Welcome to the show.
How are you, Bridget?

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.667
-Great band.
You sound great, guys.

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:12.033
-They're wonderful.
You're wonderful.

00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:13.501
Congratulations on the show.
-Thank you.

00:01:13.501 --> 00:01:16.934
-I've heard you describe it as
a coming-of-middle-age story,

00:01:16.934 --> 00:01:19.067
which is really a beautiful way
to describe it.

00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:20.968
What was it like
working on the show?

00:01:20.968 --> 00:01:22.467
What made you want to
work on this show?

00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:25.267
-Well, HBO gave me a deal,
which was number one.

00:01:25.267 --> 00:01:27.767
-That's a good start.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:29.934
-I don't know.
Our writers, Paul and Hannah,

00:01:29.934 --> 00:01:33.601
pitched this idea about a woman
who moves home to her hometown

00:01:33.601 --> 00:01:35.767
to take care of her sister
who has now passed,

00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:37.834
and then they have this thing
called choir practice,

00:01:37.834 --> 00:01:39.767
which is like a fun, little
place where they all go sing.

00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:41.701
And for me --
Sorry, I'm out of breath.

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:42.767
I just walked from here to here.

00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:43.934
You guys don't know
what that's like.

00:01:43.934 --> 00:01:45.267
[ Laughter ]

00:01:45.267 --> 00:01:48.300
I literally -- In my apartment
I walk from the couch

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.400
to the bathroom, back to the
couch, to the liquor cabinet,

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.801
back to the couch,
back to the bathroom.

00:01:53.801 --> 00:01:56.434
So that was a stretch.
Anyway, where were we?

00:01:56.434 --> 00:01:57.534
The show, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:57.534 --> 00:01:58.767
-The thing we're here
to talk about.

00:01:58.767 --> 00:01:59.968
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.968 --> 00:02:02.634
Yeah, so it's a woman,
she's just in her 40s,

00:02:02.634 --> 00:02:04.501
kind of gliding through life,
and I can relate to that,

00:02:04.501 --> 00:02:07.767
because I did that from
my late 20s to my mid-40s.

00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:12.167
-So your journey in New York --
You became this very well-known

00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:15.133
cabaret performer, but sort of
your first intro to performing

00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:18.434
in New York was doing
karaoke nights at a club.

00:02:18.434 --> 00:02:20.400
What was that like
and what were your songs?

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.968
And did right away you know,

00:02:21.968 --> 00:02:22.901
"Oh, this is what
I should be doing"?

00:02:22.901 --> 00:02:23.934
-Well, it was electric.

00:02:23.934 --> 00:02:26.234
-Yeah. I bet it was electric.
-It was.

00:02:26.234 --> 00:02:29.267
Because if there's something you
love doing and you can't do it

00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:31.634
and the only place you can do it
happens to be at a karaoke bar,

00:02:31.634 --> 00:02:34.701
you are going to go wild and
especially if you like alcohol.

00:02:34.701 --> 00:02:37.434
[ Laughter ]
And your friend's a bartender.

00:02:37.434 --> 00:02:39.968
My friend Alan used to just do
one of these

00:02:39.968 --> 00:02:42.200
and then I'd sing "Piece of My
Heart" and "You Oughta Know,"

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:45.100
get up on the bar, rip open
my top and make some friends.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:46.100
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:48.267
And then you brought
that sort of

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:50.567
take-no-prisoners approach
to your cabaret act.

00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:53.267
You become very close with
audience members.

00:02:53.267 --> 00:02:56.467
This is one of many photos
that really give a sense of

00:02:56.467 --> 00:02:58.000
what you do on stage.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.167
For those who can't tell
right now,

00:02:59.167 --> 00:03:01.033
there's an audience member
beneath you.

00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:03.100
[ Laughter ]

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.033
-Yeah.

00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:07.400
Look, I think that
music and the arts

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.367
are about
connecting with people.

00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:10.667
[ Laughter ]

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:13.334
And, you know, smiling
from the inside out.

00:03:13.334 --> 00:03:15.868
[ Laughter ]

00:03:15.868 --> 00:03:18.801
-So you were the youngest of
six, and -- There it is.

00:03:18.801 --> 00:03:20.100
-I'm proud of myself.
-Yeah.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.067
-I did that, and now I'm on HBO.

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:24.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:28.434
-So, I am wondering,
as the youngest of six,

00:03:28.434 --> 00:03:30.033
you must know you're funny

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:31.834
when you can make
older siblings laugh.

00:03:31.834 --> 00:03:33.601
-Well, okay.
I'm the youngest of six.

00:03:33.601 --> 00:03:35.267
Bridget, Brock, Brian,
Brooke, Brad, Brenton.

00:03:35.267 --> 00:03:40.901
And my family like has
one of those relentless

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:43.133
senses of humor, like, where
they make fun of each other

00:03:43.133 --> 00:03:44.601
until someone cries.

00:03:44.601 --> 00:03:46.801
And when you're the youngest,
that's you.

00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:49.267
[ Laughter ]
So they used to call me Fang

00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:52.000
'cause I had one front tooth,
and then they called me Lurch

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:53.534
'cause I had broad shoulders.

00:03:53.534 --> 00:03:55.200
I was a talented swimmer.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.667
You know,
from "The Addams Family."

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:58.334
Of course, you call me that on
the street, I'll cut you [bleep]

00:03:58.334 --> 00:04:00.400
Thank you so much.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.400
-Family only.
That's family only.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.634
I've also read that
part of this show

00:04:04.634 --> 00:04:06.601
is a love letter to your mother.

00:04:06.601 --> 00:04:10.234
Was she someone, growing up,
that made you laugh?

00:04:10.234 --> 00:04:13.367
-My mom is, like, one of those
accidentally funny people.

00:04:13.367 --> 00:04:16.968
You know, we grew up in Kansas,
and by the time she had me --

00:04:16.968 --> 00:04:18.734
I'm the sixth kid --
she sort of just like,

00:04:18.734 --> 00:04:20.467
didn't care anymore, right?

00:04:20.467 --> 00:04:22.934
So we would go to the grocery
store in the dead of winter

00:04:22.934 --> 00:04:25.167
and she would have like
a paper-thin nightgown on

00:04:25.167 --> 00:04:27.901
with no bra, because why?
[ Laughter ]

00:04:27.901 --> 00:04:30.634
She has these beautiful long --
now she just has one,

00:04:30.634 --> 00:04:33.667
but before she had two --
these long, low-hanging [bleep]

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:35.133
we call beaver tails.
-Oh, beaver tails.

00:04:35.133 --> 00:04:37.300
-So she was just beaver tails
about town.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:38.567
[ Laughter ]

00:04:38.567 --> 00:04:41.133
We would go into the Food 4 Less
and she's just like,

00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:42.834
"Take a shopping cart
and go get yourself

00:04:42.834 --> 00:04:44.701
some cereal and ice cream."
I would be like, "Okay."

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:46.667
And she would just go
flapping down the aisles.

00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:49.334
[ Laughter ]
Living her best life.

00:04:49.334 --> 00:04:52.934
-You have something that I think
is true for a lot of actors that

00:04:52.934 --> 00:04:54.868
is maybe hard for people
to grasp, because I think

00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:57.601
a lot of people assume the first
time they see you in a movie,

00:04:57.601 --> 00:04:58.801
you've got it made.
-Yeah.

00:04:58.801 --> 00:05:01.100
-But you were in
the "Sex and the City" movie.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.133
So you were in a movie a lot of
people saw, but you were not --

00:05:04.133 --> 00:05:06.901
At that point,
you were still waiting tables.

00:05:06.901 --> 00:05:08.467
-Well, yeah. I did get a break.

00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:10.868
I got to be in
the "Sex and the City" movie,

00:05:10.868 --> 00:05:13.000
which was, you know,
a worldwide hit.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.334
But luckily, I also got
the premium shifts

00:05:15.334 --> 00:05:17.701
at my restaurant job,
just up the street

00:05:17.701 --> 00:05:20.300
from Lincoln Plaza Cinemas,
where large groups of women

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:23.234
would go see it, and then they
would come to see us afterwards.

00:05:23.234 --> 00:05:27.367
And they'd be like, "Oh, my God,
oh, my God.

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:29.801
You type like a mother[bleep]
right?

00:05:29.801 --> 00:05:31.734
[ Laughter ]
I'm like, "That's me."

00:05:31.734 --> 00:05:33.133
And they're like,
"Okay, now we'd like --"

00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:34.534
you know, and then it was over.

00:05:34.534 --> 00:05:37.934
So New York, you know, lifts you
up and then it spits you out

00:05:37.934 --> 00:05:39.767
and kicks you down the stairs,
doesn't it?

00:05:39.767 --> 00:05:41.834
-It certainly does.
One of the things that must be

00:05:41.834 --> 00:05:44.100
fascinating about doing
a cabaret show in New York,

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.100
especially one that becomes
well-regarded,

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:48.934
you go into the audience
to interact with people.

00:05:48.934 --> 00:05:51.834
And sometimes you might lay eyes
on famous faces.

00:05:51.834 --> 00:05:54.267
Is it only in that moment, or
does someone backstage tell you

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:56.501
ahead of time, "Celebrity A, B,
and C are here"?

00:05:56.501 --> 00:05:58.234
-Well, I usually know
who the famous people are

00:05:58.234 --> 00:06:00.467
because they always wait until
the last minute to get tickets

00:06:00.467 --> 00:06:02.667
and it's sold out, so I have to
give them my free tickets,

00:06:02.667 --> 00:06:04.200
which isn't that funny.
-Yeah.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.501
-Anyway, but this particular
night, one particular night,

00:06:07.501 --> 00:06:09.634
I was walking around the
audience and there was a table

00:06:09.634 --> 00:06:11.567
that was missing and I was,
you know, going around,

00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:12.901
I was singing this song
about [bleep]

00:06:12.901 --> 00:06:14.834
It's a love letter to my mom,
beaver tails and whatnot.

00:06:14.834 --> 00:06:17.667
And I turn around and I'm
getting ready to make contact

00:06:17.667 --> 00:06:20.701
with a new audience member,
and it's, like, boom,

00:06:20.701 --> 00:06:22.801
and it's Gloria Steinem
and I'm like, "Cut the track!

00:06:22.801 --> 00:06:24.968
Cut the track! Cut the track!

00:06:24.968 --> 00:06:27.968
I'm not going to hug
Gloria Steinem with my [bleep]

00:06:27.968 --> 00:06:29.534
We just met."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:29.534 --> 00:06:31.267
But she loved it.
-She would've been into it.

00:06:31.267 --> 00:06:33.067
-She was into it.
-There you go.

00:06:33.067 --> 00:06:34.734
Hey, congratulations
on the show.

00:06:34.734 --> 00:06:36.467
It's really wonderful.
It's got a wonderful cast.

00:06:36.467 --> 00:06:37.934
And I can't wait
to see more of it.

00:06:37.934 --> 00:06:39.434
Thanks so much for being here.
That's Bridget Everett!

00:06:39.434 --> 00:06:40.667
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:06:40.667 --> 00:06:43.067
-New episodes of "Somebody
Somewhere" are available

00:06:43.067 --> 00:06:44.634
Sundays on HBO and HBO Max.

00:06:44.634 --> 00:06:45.968
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

